CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight
SCALE_REALISM: Real for the two captured agent instruments, but not yet a realistic serving-scale systems result: 74 Claude Code sessions / 93 Codex sessions, char/4 token proxy, L0 Mac CPU artifact, no production scheduler/cache telemetry, and no on-node chat baseline. The majority and heavy-tail claims likely hold for these traces, but the systems-level generalization needs larger multi-user traces and tokenizer/server-side validation.
RATIONALE: The measured majority result is real on the packet evidence: conservative D1 result share is CC 0.657 with LB95 0.569 and Codex 0.770 with LB95 0.748, and robustness checks include HHI 0.166/0.016 below the 0.20 flag, session medians above 0.50, top-decile result mass above 0.50, and Gini 0.863/0.749. I see no OSS collision where a shipping system already reports this tool-result vs tool-call-arg vs decode-interstitial token-mass decomposition; vLLM, SGLang/RadixAttention, LMCache, TensorRT-LLM, and FlashInfer implement or accelerate KV reuse/prefix caching rather than measuring this workload property. The s7 reconciliation is acceptable if the frozen D1 raw-span denominator and parser definitions are auditable at HEAD 8e60c29/8968a5b; on the packet, the threshold was not moved and D1 remains the most conservative denominator. Vote is yellow, not green, because systems realism is still limited by missing on-node chat contrast and lack of production-scale serving validation, even though this is more than integration glue.
